{"records":[{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ztg7pxr6ch","title":"STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency","description":"This dataset contains the original raw and analyzed data supporting the manuscript “STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency”. The study identifies STT3A as an essential regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway and a potential therapeutic target for RNF43-deficient cancers. Included data were generated from key experiments, including Wnt reporter assays (DDT), Western blotting (WB), and CRISPR screen, and other experiments central to the study","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-13T11:26:58.874Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztg7pxr6ch","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztg7pxr6ch"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengjin","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pnbjdtdkfg","title":"A genome-scale gain-of-function CRISPR screen in CD8 T cells identifies proline metabolism as a means to enhance CAR-T therapy ","description":"CyTOF raw data.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-14T13:21:30.568Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pnbjdtdkfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pnbjdtdkfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lupeng","last_name":"Ye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jonathan","last_name":"Park","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sidi","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5z7ppzwpcw","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 screening of the deubiquitinase subfamily identifies USP49 as a protein stabilizer of PAX9 and MSX1 regulating odontogenesis","description":"Deubiquitination of proteins by DUBs has emerged as a key regulator of protein stability and has been shown to be critically involved in signal transduction cascades. However, the function and regulation of individual DUBs are not well understood at the molecular level. Several screening approaches to identify DUBs and their target protein substrates in diverse cellular functions have been reported previously. For example, shRNA/siRNA libraries targeting different DUBs or overexpression of ectopically introduced DUBs have been used to investigate the impact of DUB function on target substrate protein stability. These conventional methods have several limitations, such as the limitation of RNAi or shRNA-based methods to transcripts, and these techniques often result in partial repression or restoration of gene functions in a few generations, with temporary effects.  The results obtained from these methods may not be accurate and might lead to misinterpretations of the data. To overcome this, we developed a loss-of-function study based on individually generated single cell-derived biallelic DUB knockout clones in mammalian cells and provide an alternate and improved model to explore the molecular mechanism of DUBs.\n\nIn this study, we used CRISPR/Cas9 (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats/CRISPR-associated) mediated DUB knockout screening kit based on genome-scale knockout of the entire set of genes that encode human ubiquitin-specific proteases (USPs), which are the largest subfamily of DUBs (>50 genes), and then we systematically applied the panel to screen DUB candidates that might regulate PAX9 and MSX1 proteins. PAX9 and MSX1 are critical for the transition in odontogenic potential from epithelium to mesenchyme. Although genetic mutations in PAX9 and MSX1 cause tooth agenesis, the regulation of PAX9 and MSX1 protein levels by deubiquitinating enzymes (DUBs) might be a crucial factor during tooth development. Our screening system identified USP49 as a key regulator of PAX9 and MSX1 protein stability. USP49 interacts and deubiquitinates PAX9 and MSX1 protein and upregulates several odontogenic transcription factors in human dental pulp stem cells (hDPSCs). In animal models, USP49 depletion was correlated with downregulation of Pax9 and Msx1 protein expression resulting in several morphological defects in calcified tissues such as reduced dentin growth, reduced enamel space, abnormal enamel formation, and irregular mineralization. Thus, the depletion of USP49 leads to an inadequate transition from bud to cap stage in the early stages of tooth development. This novel finding suggests that the USP49-PAX9-MSX1 axis could serve as a prognostic marker and novel regulatory target in odontogenesis and can serve as a catalyst for bioengineering tooth implant research to improve clinical care.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-08T12:30:21.375Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5z7ppzwpcw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5z7ppzwpcw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Suresh","last_name":"Ramakrishna","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology","Stem Cell","Animal Development","Tooth Enamel","Tooth Development"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x29rjbpk8r","title":"Raw Data for \"A chemical screen identifies PRMT5 as a therapeutic vulnerability for paclitaxel-resistant triple-negative breast cancer\"","description":"This file contains the following raw data for \"A chemical screen identifies PRMT5 as a therapeutic vulnerability for paclitaxel-resistant triple-negative breast cancer\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-24T09:06:29.878Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x29rjbpk8r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x29rjbpk8r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Juan","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sheyu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biologicals"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3888sct8c5","title":"Polysaccharide utilization loci in Bacteroides determine population fitness and community-level interactions. Feng et al.","description":"Polysaccharide utilization loci (PULs) in the human gut microbiome have critical roles in shaping human health and ecological dynamics. We develop a CRISPR-FnCpf1-RecT genome-editing tool to study 23 PULs in the highly abundant species B. uniformis (BU). We identify the glycan-degrading functions of multiple PULs and elucidate transcriptional coordination between PULs that enables the population to adapt to the loss of PULs. Exploiting a pooled BU mutant barcoding strategy, we demonstrate that the in vitro fitness and the colonization ability of BU in the murine gut is enhanced by deletion of specific PULs and modulated by glycan availability. We show that BU PULs can mediate complex glycan-dependent interactions with butyrate producers that depend on the mechanism of degradation and the butyrate producer glycan utilizing ability. In sum, PULs are major determinants of community dynamics and butyrate production and can provide a selective advantage or disadvantage depending on the nutritional landscape. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-03T09:17:29.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3888sct8c5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3888sct8c5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JUN","last_name":"FENG","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Next Generation Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":91947,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":118,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":91947,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":91690,"restricted":49,"embargoed":208},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1493},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":454},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":362},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":326},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":317},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":308},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":288},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":279},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":228},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":175}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":89653},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1749},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":145},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":92},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":59},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f6